On 29.03.2011 05:34, karadoc wrote:
> [Note: this is a long post. I had a few breaks while writing it.
> Please don't feel like you need to read it all at once!]

Done. Here we go... :-)

> [Balance]

> I've decreased prob of loading bones from 1/3 to 1/5, and increased
> prob of saving bones from 1-(1 / (1 + dlvl/4)) to 1-(1 / (1 + dlvl/
> 3)).

Hmm.. - I see your point if you're playing alone on your own machine.
On a public server that might not be the right direction to go.

> I've made it possible to refresh knowledge of spells sooner (ie.
> rereading spellbooks to make knowledge of the spell keener); changed
> from 1000 remaining turns to 5000 remaining turns. I've also added a
> visual indicator on the spell list to show which spells can be
> refreshed.

I like both ideas. (The are some more prominent issues with Vanilla's
spell system, though.)

> I've decreased xp required for lv 12 and above. It use to be have exp
> growth up to lv20, then linear. Now it is exp to lv12, then quadratic.
> The reason for this change is that I've found it becomes pretty much
> impossible to level-up after the low teens (apart from direct level
> gain from potions/wraiths/foobus). The effects of this change are
> pretty minor... it still takes a ridiculous number of kills to level
> up. [...]

The message in Vanilla might be; "Go waste your potions of gain level!"
(or use the other ways to level up; wraihts, foocubi) if you like to
become a hight XL character. There's really no need to be able to level
up by kills.

> Now.. onto the more major changes. [...]
> [...] I want to make the
> end game a bit harder, and possibly make the early game a bit easier
> to compensate. So here's what I've done (still in order of smallest to
> largest):
> 
> I've reduced the protection bought from priests. Instead of turning
> probabilistic at +9 and maxing out at +20, it now turns probabilistic
> at +5 and maxes out at +14; however, the probability has been
> increased a bit. Also lowered initial range form 2-5 to 1-3. So
> typically this protection will now give around +5 or 6 instead of +9.

I generally don't like those arbitrary values (9/20 vs. 2/14).

What I'd like would probably easiest be described by a linear function;
Success probability for the 1st to 11th point: 100%, 95%, 90%, ... 50%,
then either continuing linear to 0% for the 21st point of protection,
or asymptotic (but I think the linearity is sufficient).

I'd also change the number of granted AC points per donation from 2-4
points for the first and 1 point for all the next steps to something
different; like 1-4, 1-4, 1-3, 1-3, 1-2, 1-2, 1, 1, ..., 1 point, i.e.
not focussing the huge gain on the very first donation.

> 
> I've changed the formula for the maximum level of random monsters from
> (clvl + dlvl)/2 to (clvl + turns/1000 +1 + 2*dlvl)/4. I think this
> change requires a bit of explaining; so bear with me. This formula
> describes how tough the monsters you meet are likely to be.

I like the thought behind that proposal, and basically agree with it.
But I have two issues with it as well. First, the assumption...

> [...] A typical ascension is around 35,000
> turns. Based on that, I estimate that my formula will make the early
> game a little bit easier, and the mid-to-late game harder.

...is not appropriate. I recall that at the time when I started to get
able to ascend, my games required rather 80k turns (130k turns if stuck
with teleportitis, but usually not less than 70k turns); and I've read
here occasionally confirmations of those values from new ascenders. So
the number of turns should IMO be increased in your formula. The second
point is a principle one; I think you should be able to stay on a level
and wait, e.g. to recover from attacks, without too much penalty;
especially weak character classes will get a disadvantage from that
penalty, while the fast fighter classes may not even notice the effect.

> [MC, magic cancellation]
> 
> As I've said, I think the late game is a bit too easy. In particular I
> don't like that the hero is essentially immune to all the special
> attacks that the late-game monsters can deal. I think it's a shame
> that some of the late-game monsters essentially never get to use their
> special attacks. For example, wraiths and vampires are laughable
> because their level drain attack almost always fails.

Well, that depends on whether you have MC=3 or not. I often travel with
just a MC=2 cloak because of its other good properties. With or without
MC=3, handling level draining monsters is one proficiency that you have
to learn and apply. So, even if you'd make MC=3 the same probability as
MC=2 there wouldn't really be that huge a difference as long as you don't
play the game completely careless.

Janis


